narration: 23 lines, 2:27 spoken (say Karen (Premium) at 202 wpm)
   0.1s  Two companies. One question neither can answer.
   7.7s  Neither is allowed to see the other's customer records.
  12.8s  Today: a clean-room vendor, a contract, six figures, and weeks.
  22.6s  The data leaves both buildings to answer one question.
  28.9s  WebMCP lets a page hand an agent a narrow set of verbs.
  36.1s  So the question can travel instead of the data.
  42.3s  Airlock: two ordinary web apps, on two different origins.
  51.0s  A marketer asks in plain language. Nothing is uploaded.
  55.7s  The tool that crosses the boundary is not registered yet.
  61.4s  Nothing to call — and no wording brings it into existence.
  68.7s  Asking for the records directly is refused outright.
  75.2s  Approval is a business decision. A person makes it on each side.
  80.9s  The request crosses to the publisher's console as a tool call.
